 text-overflow:ellipsis;direction:rtl;-webkit-user-select:all;user-select:all}
/* The path reads left to right; only the CUT comes from the right. Without this
   the bidi algorithm hands the leading `~/` to the enclosing right-to-left run
   and draws it at the far end, so a short path reads `work/shopfront~/`. */
.pt>span{direction:ltr;unicode-bidi:embed}
.pc{font-size:11px;color:var(--dim);white-space:nowrap;flex:none}
/* Characters never shrink to fit: they wrap. Five agents in one checkout flow
   onto as many lines as they need at full size and the band grows to hold them.
   Width follows how much there is to say, and 2.7.0 said that as "a sentence
   gets two of the floor's columns, a quiet agent one". Two columns was enough
   while a cell was a branch, some chips and at most one sentence. It stopped
   being enough in 2.9.0, which gave a cell a subagent TREE and a derived line
   of its own: an agent with a tree came out twice as tall as the four beside
   it, the space next to the tree was empty, and a sentence squeezed into two
   narrow columns ran to six lines while its neighbour's ran to one. Five
   figures on one strip read as a ragged wall rather than a row.
   So the rule is the same rule, taken to its end: an agent carrying a BLOCK —
   a sentence it said, work it declared, or subagents standing under it — takes
   the WHOLE strip, and everything short wraps side by side. Full width is what
   makes a block short: the same sentence is one line across the strip and six
   in a 232px column, so this packs the page rather than stretching it.
   Flex and not a column template, because a wrapped flex line divides the width
   between the cells that are actually on it: four short figures get a quarter of
   the strip each, and one gets all of it, without a track count fixed for the
   whole floor by the widest row in it. */
.floor{grid-area:floor;position:relative;display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;
 align-items:flex-start;row-gap:10px}
/* The ground. Drawn per plot with no column gap between plots, so the segments
   join into one strip, and set exactly at the height of the figures' feet. Full
   width and at the feet are both load-bearing: the strip is what says these
   figures are in one checkout, and the feet are where the contact shadow already
   says the figure is standing. Neither of them may give way to make room for a
   cell's writing — `--say` is what keeps the writing off this line. */
.floor>div{--head:0px;position:relative;flex:1 1 232px;min-width:0;
 padding-right:14px;padding-top:var(--head)}
.floor>div[data-block]{flex-basis:100%}
.floor>div::before{content:"";position:absolute;left:0;right:0;
 top:calc(var(--head) + var(--fh));height:2px;background:var(--ground)}
/* Room above the heads for the arc that joins two sessions which wrote one
   file — declared, and only on the strips that have one. It used to be taken
   from wherever happened to be above, which since the figures sit about thirty
   pixels from the top of their band meant the project's heading.
   Per PLOT and not once for the whole strip: a strip whose figures wrap onto a
   second line has a second row of heads, and headroom taken at the top of the
   floor is no headroom at all for those — the arc was clamped against the top of
   the floor, so between two figures on the second line it lifted up over the
   content of the first and drew its label across it. The ground line moves down
   with the headroom, or the figures would stand above their own ground. */
.floor.tied>div{--head:22px}
/* A checkout with nobody in it still has ground, and something is standing to
   land off it. A bare strip says that; dropping the row would say nothing. */
.floor.bare{min-height:calc(var(--fh) + 2px)}
